window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] = window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] || {};
window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][137] = "<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> ai · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C25 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AI · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Google</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Can we use a language model to grade the answers of another language model?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, it saves money,&quot; or &quot;no, it can't be trusted.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Says to calibrate it against human judgments.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Measures the judge's precision and recall on real failures, explains why accuracy misleads with rare failures, names its biases, and designs a triage with human samples.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; nothing here describes any real system. Yes, with care: it is a useful tool and a poor final authority until measured. <em>Calibrate first.</em> Take a sample of outputs judged by trained people, then have the model judge the same outputs against the same rubric, and compare. Do not stop at agreement. Suppose, of 1,000 outputs, humans find 100 bad. The model flags 80 as bad, 70 of them right and 10 wrong. Precision is 70 ÷ 80 = 87.5%; recall is 70 ÷ 100 = 70%; overall accuracy is (70 + 890) ÷ 1,000 = 96%. The 96% sounds excellent and hides that the judge lets 30% of the bad outputs through. <em>Known weaknesses.</em> Preference for longer or more fluent answers; trouble verifying facts it was not given; sensitivity to the order of answers shown to it; leniency toward outputs that resemble its own style; mistakes correlated with the system being graded. <em>Make it better.</em> Give it the source documents and ask claim-by-claim checks; use a separate check per rubric criterion instead of one overall score; include examples of subtle failures in its instructions; use a different model family from the one being graded where possible; randomize answer order. Recalibrate after each change. <em>Use it as triage.</em> Run it on everything, send the flagged items to humans along with a random sample of the passed ones to estimate the miss rate, and track that miss rate over time. Do not gate a launch on the judge alone for high-stakes use. <em>Company note.</em> Google is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of generated-answer evaluation prompts <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Google data or practice is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c25-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C25-06</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> ai · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C25 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AI · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Meta</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Suggested replies in a messaging product are accepted 38% of the time. Is the feature working?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, 38% is good adoption.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Questions whether acceptance measures quality and asks about errors.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Measures accepted-and-wrong, computes the break-even error rate from time saved and cost of error, reads effects by week, checks sensitive contexts and long-term effects with a holdout, and sets a launch rule.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; nothing here describes any real product. Acceptance tells me people tapped the suggestion; it does not tell me the suggestion was right, appropriate or helpful. It could reflect a useful feature or one users trust too much. <em>Check what was accepted.</em> Sample accepted suggestions and have reviewers judge them: wrong facts, wrong tone for the relationship, inappropriate for the moment. Suppose 5% are bad. Track later corrections, follow-up messages and complaints too. <em>Break-even.</em> Suppose an accepted suggestion saves 18 seconds and a bad one costs the user 6 minutes (360 seconds) in repair or embarrassment. The break-even error rate is 18 ÷ 360 = 5%, since the saving applies to every acceptance and each bad one adds 360 seconds of repair. At 5% bad among accepted the feature is a wash for the user; below it helps, above it hurts. So 38% acceptance with 5% bad is no evidence of value, and I would want the error rate well below 5% with an interval below the break-even. <em>Time and learning.</em> Read acceptance by week: early novelty inflates it, and users learn which suggestions to trust, so it may fall. Compare with a holdout over several weeks on messaging volume, satisfaction, and relationship-sensitive outcomes (conversations that end abruptly after a suggested reply). <em>Context risk.</em> Replies in sensitive contexts (condolences, conflict, health, money) cost more when wrong; exclude or restrict suggestions there, and measure by context. <em>Launch rule.</em> Accepted-and-wrong at most 2% overall and near zero in sensitive contexts; complaints no higher than the holdout; no drop in conversation health measures; and monitoring after launch. What would change my view: evidence that bad suggestions concentrate in a segment or topic, which I can remove while keeping the benefit elsewhere. <em>Company note.</em> Meta is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of feature-experiment prompts <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Meta data or practice is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c25-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C25-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> ai · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C25 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AI · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Amazon</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Price an AI assistant add-on for sellers on a marketplace so that it makes money.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Charge a flat monthly fee similar to competitors.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Computes cost per task and margin and considers heavy users.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes margin by segment, shows how an average hides losses, designs allowance and overage, routing and caps, tests price elasticity, and ties price to seller value.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; nothing here describes any real product. A flat fee is attractive to sellers and risky because AI cost scales with use. <em>Cost by segment.</em> Suppose a seller's average use is 150 tasks a month at $0.04 each ($6) plus $1.50 of other cost: $7.50. A heavy seller (10% of sellers) runs 900 tasks: $36 plus $1.50: $37.50. At a flat $15 a month: average margin $7.50 (50%), heavy margin −$22.50. Blended cost: 0.9 × $7.50 + 0.1 × $37.50 = $10.50, so the margin is $4.50, 30%. <em>Redesign.</em> Include 400 tasks a month and charge $0.05 per additional task. The heavy seller then pays $15 + 500 × $0.05 = $40 against a cost of $37.50: a small positive margin. Blended revenue: 0.9 × $15 + 0.1 × $40 = $17.50, margin $17.50 − $10.50 = $7.00, 40%. Average sellers see no change. <em>Other levers.</em> Route simple tasks to cheaper models (cost per task falls; test quality by route); cache repeated outputs; shorten prompts; offer a higher-priced tier for power users; consider pricing by outcome where value is measurable (a listing that sells). <em>Value anchor.</em> Price relative to what the seller gains: if the assistant saves a seller five hours a month or lifts conversion, a $15 fee is small; measure that benefit and use it in the message. <em>Test.</em> Price experiments by cohort for elasticity (conversion, churn, usage), watch complaints about overage, and monitor margin by cohort monthly. Keep in mind the marketplace context: sellers who feel nickel-and-dimed may leave, so make allowances generous enough that most never see overage. <em>Company note.</em> Amazon is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of pricing and unit-economics prompts <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Amazon data or pricing is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c25-08\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C25-08</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> ai · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C25 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AI · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Stripe</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> An AI agent handles payment disputes and refunds for support. How do you decide how much autonomy to give it, and when to give it more?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Let it handle small refunds on its own.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Sets approval levels by amount and error cost.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Defines promotion evidence with sample sizes, per-band limits, demotion triggers, audit and reversibility, and weighs the economics against the cost of error.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; nothing here describes any real system. Autonomy should be earned per action and per amount band, with evidence and a way back (FW-C25-05). <em>Start low.</em> At first the agent drafts the decision and the reply; a person approves each one. This gathers data on how often the agent is right, for each type and amount. <em>Promotion evidence.</em> To promote small refunds (under $20) to autonomous, require: at least 300 supervised cases in that band with no errors (the upper 95% bound on the error rate is then about 1%, by the rule of three), or about 600 for a 0.5% bound; no severe incident; low override rate by reviewers; reversibility (a refund can be reversed or limited); and monitoring and a kill switch in place. Each amount band and each case type (duplicate charge, item not received, fraud claim) earns its own evidence; reliability on $10 duplicate charges says little about $500 fraud disputes. <em>Limits that bound the risk.</em> Caps on amount per refund and per customer per month, a daily total, a rule that suspicious patterns go to a person, an audit log of every action with the evidence used, and a short reversal window. <em>Demotion.</em> A written rule: if the error rate in a week exceeds 1% on at least 100 cases, or any severe incident occurs (a refund to the wrong party, a fraud loss above a set amount), drop that band back to approval and investigate. <em>Economics.</em> Suppose 50,000 refunds a month, and automating a refund saves $2 of handling: $100,000 a month. If 0.5% are wrong at an average cost of $30, errors cost 250 × $30 = $7,500 a month, a small fraction of the savings, so promotion pays when the evidence supports a 0.5% bound. For larger amounts the cost of an error rises with the amount, and the evidence bar rises with it. <em>Fraud and abuse.</em> Refund agents attract manipulation (a customer's message instructing the agent); treat message text as untrusted, never let it change limits, and require extra checks for risky signals. <em>Company note.</em> Stripe is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of automation-with-money prompts <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Stripe data or practice is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c25-09\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C25-09</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> ai · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C25 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AI · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Your company sells an AI product in several regions. How do you approach regulation and compliance?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Ask legal to check it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Classifies by risk and notes regional differences.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Triages each use by risk tier and market, uses current dates carefully (including deferrals), builds documentation and transparency into the product, and sets owners and launch implications, without giving legal advice.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I am not a lawyer and rules change quickly, so I'd involve legal and policy from the start and check current texts <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>. My job as PM is to give them a clear picture and build the product to meet what they decide. <em>Triage (FW-C25-07).</em> List each AI use and each market. Classify by risk tier: prohibited uses; high-risk uses (employment decisions, credit, education, essential services, safety components); limited-risk uses with transparency duties (chatbots must disclose that the user is dealing with a machine; generated content may need labeling); minimal risk (a spam filter). Take an example: a customer-service chatbot is typically limited-risk, so the main duty is disclosure; a resume-screening feature is typically high-risk, with obligations on documentation, human oversight, data quality, and testing for bias. <em>Know the dates, and read deferrals carefully.</em> In the European Union, for example, prohibited practices have applied since February 2025, obligations for general-purpose model providers since August 2025, and transparency duties for chatbots and generated content from 2 August 2026 (with a transition to 2 December 2026 for machine-readable marking by generative systems already on the market); the obligations for high-risk systems, originally due 2 August 2026, were deferred by Regulation (EU) 2026/1744 to 2 December 2027 for stand-alone systems and 2 August 2028 for AI embedded in regulated products <code>[V: Regulation (EU) 2026/1744 on EUR-Lex; legal summaries for the dates]</code>. A deferral is not an exemption: the work of documentation and testing takes time, and transparency duties may already apply. <em>Build in what is required.</em> Disclosure that the user is interacting with an AI; labeling of generated content; human review for significant decisions; records of the system's purpose, data, evaluation results, known limits and monitoring (a model card or equivalent); logs for audit; data protection (what is collected, retention, user rights). <em>Other regions.</em> In the United States, existing consumer-protection and anti-discrimination laws apply and some states and cities have AI-specific rules <code>[V: US: April 2023 joint statement by EEOC, CFPB, FTC and DOJ on existing law; NYC Local Law 144; other state rules not enumerated]</code>; in India, data protection law, non-binding AI governance guidelines and binding IT Rules amendments on synthetic content apply <code>[V: India: non-binding MeitY guidelines and binding IT Rules amendments, per law-firm summaries, 2026]</code>; in China, specific rules cover recommendation algorithms and generative services <code>[V: China: algorithm, deep synthesis and generative AI rules, per law-firm summaries]</code>. A feature may need different designs, or a delayed launch, in different markets. <em>Operate it.</em> Assign owners (legal, policy, security, product), put compliance gates into the launch checklist, and review when rules change.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c25-10\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C25-10</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> ai · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C25 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AI · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> What is the moat of an AI writing assistant if the underlying models keep improving?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Our AI is better than the competition's.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names data, distribution or workflow as sources of advantage.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Tests each source for durability, separates what compounds from what does not, notes that model access is not a moat, and says what to build toward.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd be honest: the model itself is unlikely to be the moat if others can access the same or better models. So I'd test each candidate source with the sources-of-advantage test (FW-C18-05). <em>Weak sources.</em> A clever prompt (easy to copy and to replace); a thin wrapper around a general model; the choice of model (rivals can switch); novelty. <em>Possible sources.</em> (1) <em>Distribution:</em> if the assistant sits inside the tools where people already write (email, documents), it reaches users that a standalone rival must win. (2) <em>Workflow integration and switching costs:</em> the more it holds a person's or team's templates, style guides, past documents and approvals, the harder it is to leave. (3) <em>Proprietary data and feedback loops:</em> data that others lack and that improves with use (what edits users make to suggestions, house style) can compound, though a stronger general model may erase the advantage if the task does not need your data. (4) <em>Evaluation capability:</em> a mature test set and monitoring let me improve faster and ship safely; a new entrant starts from nothing. (5) <em>Trust and accountability:</em> in domains with costly errors (legal, medical, finance), a record of reliability, audit trails and clear responsibility matters. (6) <em>Cost position:</em> routing and caching deliver the same quality for less, which allows a better price or margin. <em>What compounds.</em> Workflow depth, data from use and trust compound; prompts and model choice do not. So I'd invest in becoming part of how teams write: shared style guides, review flows, integrations, and an eval that makes each model upgrade safe to adopt. <em>What I'd watch.</em> Whether competitors with the same models match our quality; whether the platforms we depend on add the feature natively; and whether our retention comes from habit and integration or from novelty.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c26-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C26-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product-sense · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C26 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How do you decide whether a new idea is worth pursuing?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd check whether the market is big and the idea is good.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Starts from the problem and uses criteria.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Scores with evidence quality, names the assumptions behind the score, compares alternatives and decides what to test first.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I start with the problem, not the idea: who has it, how often, how painful it is, what they do today and what they spend on it. Then I score the idea on a few criteria: severity and frequency, how many people have it, willingness to pay or switch, our advantage in doing it, feasibility, differentiation and fit with strategy. For each score I note the evidence: strong if I have observed behavior or a payment, medium if from interviews or surveys, weak if it's opinion or analogy, and I discount the weak ones. That gives a ranking by what I know, not by what I hope. &quot;The scorecard doesn't decide; it tells me what to test. I list the assumptions behind the top ideas, find the one that would sink the idea if it were false and about which I know the least, usually that people will pay, and design the cheapest test that could prove it wrong. I decide in advance what result would make me continue, stop or change direction.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Problem first; criteria with evidence quality; assumptions; a test with pre-set rules.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c26-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C26-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product-sense · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C26 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> What is a minimum viable product, and what is it not?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;The first version with the basic features.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> The smallest thing that tests an assumption.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Links the MVP to a specific hypothesis, gives forms that are not software, says what it is not (a poor product, a small launch), and names a success rule.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;An MVP is the smallest thing you can build or do that lets you test the riskiest assumption about an idea and learn from real behavior. It is defined by the question it answers, not by how many features it has. So it may not be software at all: a landing page with a price and a deposit tests demand; a video shows whether people understand and want it; a concierge version, where you do the service by hand for a few customers, tests value and economics; a Wizard-of-Oz version looks automated and is staffed behind the scenes; a prototype tests usability. &quot;What it is not: a bad product shipped because time ran out; a small launch with no hypothesis; or a demo that cannot fail. A good MVP has a hypothesis ('students will put down a $5 deposit for a $40-a-week meal kit'), a measure, a threshold set before the test and a decision. If the test can't change your mind, it isn't an MVP; it's a demonstration.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> The question defines it; non-software forms; what it is not; a threshold.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c26-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C26-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product-sense · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C26 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> You have an idea for a service that matches volunteers with local charities that need help for a few hours. How would you validate it cheaply?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Build an app and see if people sign up.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names assumptions and a cheap test.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Ranks assumptions, designs a test with measure, threshold and sample size, uses a concierge stage, and handles both sides of the marketplace.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;It's a two-sided idea, so I'd list assumptions for each side. Volunteers: they want short, flexible opportunities and will show up (value; uncertain). Charities: they have small tasks, can describe them, and will use a new channel (value; uncertain). Viability: matching by hand can be made cheap enough, and there is a way to fund it. The riskiest is probably whether charities will post tasks and whether volunteers will show up for them, since a marketplace with no supply or no demand is nothing (C19). &quot;Cheapest test: a concierge version. I'd contact 15 charities, ask for a list of tasks they can't staff, and match volunteers by hand through a messaging group of 100 people from one neighborhood. Measure: tasks posted per charity, the share of tasks filled within a week and the show-up rate. Thresholds, set in advance: proceed if at least 10 of 15 charities post a task and at least 60% of tasks are filled with 80% show-up; stop if fewer than 5 charities post. For a landing-page test on the volunteer side, to estimate a sign-up rate near 5% within ±2 points I'd need about 450 visitors (1.96² × 0.05 × 0.95 ÷ 0.02²). Then I'd look at what the manual work costs per match, to see what must be automated, and keep the geography small so that both sides find each other.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Both sides; the riskiest assumption; a concierge test with thresholds; economics.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c26-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C26-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product-sense · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C26 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How do you tell whether a product has product-market fit?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;When lots of people use it and it's growing.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names retention and a disappointment survey.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Reads the retention curve shape, the survey by segment, organic growth and willingness to pay, and distinguishes false signals (waiting lists, launch spikes, paid dependence, incentives).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I look for a pattern, not one number. First, retention: plot cohorts and see whether the curve flattens at a non-zero level. If it levels at, say, 25%, a core of users keeps coming back; if it falls to zero, there's no fit. Second, a survey asking how users would feel if they could no longer use the product, with 'very disappointed' as the strongest answer; a commonly cited threshold is about 40% <code>[V: Sean Ellis survey and 40% benchmark, as described in Rahul Vohra's First Round article]</code>, and I'd read it by segment, since an average can hide a group with strong fit. Third, organic growth: users arriving and inviting others without being paid. Fourth, usage frequency in the target segment and willingness to pay. Fifth, qualitative pull: customers complaining when it breaks. &quot;False signals: a waiting list or a launch spike (interest, not commitment); growth that stops when ad spend stops; usage driven by incentives; praise from friends; and averages that hide that one segment loves the product and the rest don't. The question I ask is: what happens to the numbers if marketing stops?&quot; <em>Signals.</em> A pattern of signals; segment reading; false signals.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c26-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C26-05</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product-sense · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C26 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Your landing-page smoke test got a 5% sign-up rate. Is that good?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, 5% is good.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Asks about the threshold, the audience and the sample.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes the interval, compares with a pre-set threshold, considers the commitment level, the traffic source and the price, and decides the next test.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I can't say without three things: the threshold we set before the test, the size of the sample and who the visitors were. With 300 visitors, 5% (15 sign-ups) has a standard error of about 1.3 points, so the 95% interval is roughly 2.5% to 7.5%: it could be well below a 4% bar. With 800 visitors it narrows to about ±1.5 points. So first the interval. &quot;Second, the commitment: an email sign-up is the weakest signal; a deposit or pre-order is stronger. If the page showed a price and 5% still signed up, it is more meaningful than if no price was shown. Third, the audience: visitors from a community of enthusiasts or from friends will convert more than the target segment; I'd look at conversion by source, and at who the sign-ups are. Fourth, what we compare with: we have no universal benchmark; compare with our threshold, with a control page, or with similar offers we've run. &quot;If the threshold was 4% and 1,200 visitors gave an interval of about 3.8% to 6.2%, the lower end sits just under the bar, so I'd extend the test before calling it a pass. If the whole interval were above 4%, that would be a pass on sign-ups, and I'd then test a stronger commitment: a deposit, or a concierge pilot with paying customers.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> The interval; threshold, source and commitment; the next test.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c26-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C26-06</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product-sense · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C26 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A team has worked on a new product for six months and the key metrics are flat. Should it kill, persist or pivot?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Give it more time,&quot; or &quot;kill it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Checks the criteria and the evidence.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Uses criteria set in advance, reads by segment and cohort, applies the decision table, checks sunk cost, designs the next test and a time box, and handles the team's attachment.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I'd start with the criteria the team set at the beginning: what thresholds on retention or the disappointment survey would have meant stop? If none were set, I'd write them now, before looking at more data, to avoid moving the goalposts. Then I'd read the data properly: cohort retention (flat overall may hide a segment that retains), the survey by segment, and who the retained users are. &quot;Then the decision table. Weak everywhere on tests that were well designed means kill; strong in a narrow segment means pivot to that segment; inconclusive because the test was poor means persist with a better test. Suppose retention flattens at 11% and one segment is 46% very disappointed against 18% elsewhere: that is a pivot, treated as a hypothesis with new kill criteria, say retention of 20% in that segment at week eight, and a time box of eight weeks. I'd also name sunk cost: the six months are gone either way and shouldn't weigh. &quot;For the team: I'd separate the idea from the people, thank them for what was learned, and make sure that stopping is seen as the result of a good test, not a failure.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Pre-set criteria; segment reading; the decision table; sunk cost; a time box; team care.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c26-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C26-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product-sense · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C26 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> You are asked to build a product for smallholder farmers in a region where there is little data. What do you build first?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;An app that connects farmers to buyers.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Starts with research on the users and the problem.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Researches the farmers' actual jobs and constraints (connectivity, trust, payments, language), identifies the riskiest assumption, runs an assisted concierge test through channels they already use, measures real transactions, and plans how to scale.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I'd begin with the farmers, not the app: observe and interview 20 to 30 farmers about how they sell today, to whom, at what price, how they get paid, what goes wrong and what they would pay or give up to fix it. The constraints matter: patchy connectivity, low-end phones, limited literacy in some languages, trust in middlemen and in digital payments (C09). &quot;Assumptions: farmers want a different way to sell (value); they'll use a channel they already know (usability: voice or a messaging app, not a new app); buyers will pay enough for reliable supply (viability); logistics and payment can be handled (feasibility). The riskiest is probably trust and the transaction itself: will a farmer sell through us and will the buyer pay? &quot;First build: a concierge marketplace through a phone line and a messaging group. We take produce listings from 40 farmers, match them with 6 buyers by hand, and handle payment through a method both use. Measure: listings per farmer, the share of listings sold, price compared with the farmer's usual price, repeat sales in four weeks. Thresholds: proceed if 30% of farmers (12 of 40) sell at least twice and the average price is at least 10% better than the middleman price. The learning is what to automate: matching, price information, payment. I'd decide the app only after the manual process works.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Research first; local constraints; riskiest assumption; a concierge test with thresholds.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c26-08\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C26-08</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product-sense · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C26 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> You have two zero-to-one ideas and little data on either. How do you decide which to pursue?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Pick the bigger market.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Compares on criteria and effort.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Uses evidence-adjusted scoring and a simple expected-value comparison including the cost and speed of testing, prefers the idea with faster learning when values are close, and considers reversibility and learning value.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I'd compare them on the criteria with evidence quality, then on expected value and learning speed. Suppose idea X has a 30% chance of working and a payoff of $5 million, and idea Y a 15% chance and a payoff of $12 million (all guesses, so I'd give ranges). Expected value: X is 0.30 × $5M = $1.5M; Y is 0.15 × $12M = $1.8M. After test costs of $20,000 and $30,000: $1.48M and $1.77M. On paper Y wins, but the numbers are weak guesses. &quot;So I'd add what the guesses don't capture. Learning speed: if I can test X in three weeks and Y in eight, I can resolve X sooner and move on if it fails, which has value. Evidence quality: if the success probability for Y rests on an analogy, I'd discount it. Reversibility and learning: which test also teaches something useful for the other idea or for the company? If the values are close and one test is much faster, I'd run the faster test first and keep the other in the queue; or, if the tests are cheap, run both small tests in parallel for a few weeks and decide on the results, which is often better than debating guesses.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Evidence-adjusted comparison; expected value with caution; learning speed; parallel small tests.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c27-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C27-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy-gtm · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C27 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> What makes a marketplace hard to start, and what is liquidity?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;You need both buyers and sellers.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Explains the cold start and defines liquidity.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Measures liquidity with availability, conversion and utilization, finds the binding side, and describes a start in one narrow market.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;A marketplace is worth something only if both sides are there, and each waits for the other: sellers won't list with no buyers, buyers won't come with nothing to buy. Liquidity is how likely a participant is to find a good match quickly. I measure it with the share of searches that show at least one available, relevant result, conversion once a result is shown, and how much of suppliers' capacity is used. The booking rate is the product of the first two. &quot;To start, I'd pick one narrow market (a neighborhood, a category) and make it work there before expanding. I'd find which side is short: if 60% of searches end with nothing available, supply is short; if hosts' utilization is a fifth of normal, demand is short. Then I'd add the short side in steps, checking that the other side isn't stranded.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> The cold start; measured liquidity; the binding side; a narrow start.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c27-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C27-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy-gtm · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C27 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Microsoft</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Who are the buyer, the user and the administrator of an enterprise product, and why does it matter?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;The customer is the company and the users are employees.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Distinguishes the buyer, user and administrator and what each needs.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Adds security, procurement and champions, explains the sales cycle and non-feature requirements, and says how this changes the roadmap.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; nothing here describes any real product or company. &quot;The economic buyer holds the budget and cares about cost, risk and results; the users do the work in the product and care about speed and ease; the administrators configure, secure and support it and care about control, audit and reliability. Add a champion who wants it, IT and security who can block it, and procurement and legal who set terms. Together they are the buying committee, which makes the sales cycle long: months of evaluations, reviews and approvals. &quot;It matters because a product users love can still stall if it lacks single sign-on, access control, audit logs, data controls or compliance certifications, which decide deals though they are not features users ask for. So the roadmap has three streams: user value, administrator control and enterprise requirements, and the PM balances them. I'd also design for expansion: land with a team, then grow within the account, and track net revenue retention.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> The actors; non-feature requirements; the cycle; roadmap consequence.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c27-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C27-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy-gtm · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C27 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Amazon</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How would you reduce counterfeit and low-quality sellers in a third-party marketplace without hurting good sellers?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Verify sellers and remove bad ones.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Layers prevention, detection and enforcement, and notes false positives.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Measures prevalence by sampling, sets thresholds by error cost, uses brand and buyer reports, designs appeals, and watches adaptation and fairness.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; I describe a method and claim nothing about any real company's programs <code>Inferred</code>. &quot;First measure the problem: sample 5,000 listings for expert review and find, say, 40 counterfeit, a prevalence of 0.8% with a 95% interval of about ±0.25 points; across 2 million listings that is about 16,000. Then layers. <em>Prevention:</em> verify identity and business details at onboarding, with stricter checks in high-risk categories; let brands register and gate sensitive categories. <em>Detection:</em> models on listing text, images, price and seller behavior, plus reports from buyers and brands. <em>Enforcement:</em> graduated actions (warn, limit, suspend), with appeals. <em>Incentives:</em> make counterfeits unprofitable (holds on payouts for new sellers in risky categories). &quot;Tradeoffs: stricter checks slow good new sellers and block honest ones, so I'd set thresholds by the cost of each error, use friction (extra verification) for the borderline band, and measure appeal overturn rates. Measures: prevalence (sampled), buyer complaints per 1,000 orders, time to remove, seller onboarding completion and false-suspension rate. Adversaries adapt, so I'd resample monthly and red-team the detection.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Sampled prevalence; layers; error-cost thresholds; appeals; adaptation.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c27-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C27-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy-gtm · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C27 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Stripe</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How would you reduce chargebacks on a payments product without hurting conversion?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Block more risky payments.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Separates fraud from other disputes and compares costs.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Segments chargebacks by cause, uses friction for a borderline band, computes net benefit with the cost of lost conversion, and monitors false declines.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; I describe a method and no real product's numbers <code>Inferred</code>. &quot;First understand the chargebacks: 2 million payments a month at an average $60 with a 0.45% chargeback rate is 9,000 chargebacks, and with the $15 fee about $675,000 a month. Split by cause: true fraud, 'friendly fraud' (the cardholder disputes a purchase they made), merchandise problems (not received, not as described), and unclear billing descriptors. The fixes differ: fraud needs screening; friendly fraud needs evidence and clear receipts; merchandise problems need merchant quality; descriptors need clarity. &quot;For fraud, blocking more costs good customers, so I'd add friction only for a risky band. Suppose 100,000 payments a month are in the borderline band with 4,500 of the chargebacks, and an extra verification step cuts those by 40% (1,800 × $75 = $135,000 saved) but loses 3% of the band's payments (3,000 × ($1.80 margin + $6 future value) = $23,400). Net about $111,600 a month. I'd test it on a holdout and measure conversion, chargebacks and false declines. For the other causes: clearer descriptors, receipts and delivery confirmation, and a dispute-evidence tool for merchants.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Chargebacks by cause; a friction band; net benefit with lost conversion; monitoring.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c27-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C27-05</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy-gtm · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C27 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How does an ad auction decide which ad to show and what it costs?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;The highest bidder wins.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Explains that predicted response is combined with the bid.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Works a numeric example with scores and the winner's price, explains why weighting protects users and revenue, and names calibration and ad-load guardrails.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;Each time a slot opens, the system scores each eligible ad by its bid times the predicted chance the user will respond, which is the expected revenue per impression, and shows the highest score. The winner pays the least that would keep its position, close to what the next competitor needed. Example: A bids $2.00 with a 1.0% predicted click rate (score 0.020), B bids $3.00 with 0.5% (0.015), C bids $1.50 with 1.2% (0.018). A wins. Its price per click is C's score divided by A's rate: 0.018 ÷ 0.010 = $1.80, not $2.00. B's higher bid loses because users are unlikely to click. &quot;Weighting by predicted response raises revenue (A earns $18 per thousand impressions at its price, against $15 for B at its bid), protects users from irrelevant ads and rewards good ads. It depends on calibrated predictions; if they're wrong, revenue and quality fall. Guardrails: ad load, retention and complaints.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Expected value ranking; the price rule; a numeric example; guardrails.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c27-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C27-06</h3>\n";
